-- title:
--   Height-weighted orbital integrals at diagonal classes: linearity and measurability
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a number field, with the Borel structures on $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_K)$, on the idele group and on centralisers taken to be those generated by the topologies. Fix a finite index set $s \subseteq \iota$, coefficients $c_i \in \mathbb{C}$, and $\gamma_0 \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K)$ whose off-diagonal entries $(1,0)$ and $(0,1)$ vanish and whose diagonal entries satisfy $(\gamma_0)_{00}/(\gamma_0)_{11} \neq 1$; write $\gamma$ for its image in $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_K)$ under the entrywise map induced by $K \to \mathbb{A}_K$, and let $\tau$ be a Haar measure on the centraliser of $\{\gamma\}$. Let $W(x) = -\log H(x) - \log H(wx)$, where $H$ is the adelic height (the product of the archimedean and finite local heights) and $w$ is the image in $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_K)$ of the antidiagonal Weyl element. Let $f_i$ be continuous with compact support for $i \in s$, and suppose that for each $i \in s$ and each idele $z$ the number $J_i(z)$ is a $W$-weighted orbital integral of $g \mapsto f_i(z \cdot g)$ at $\gamma$, $z$ acting by the central scalar matrix: that is, there is a non-negative measurable $\sigma$ with compact support such that $\int_{\mathrm{Cent}(\gamma)} \sigma(t x)\,d\tau(t) = 1$ whenever $f_i(z \cdot x^{-1}\gamma x) \neq 0$, and $J_i(z) = \int f_i(z \cdot x^{-1}\gamma x) \, W(x) \, \sigma(x)\, d\mu(x)$ for the adelic Haar measure $\mu$ on $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_K)$. Then for every idele $z$ the number $\sum_{i \in s} c_i J_i(z)$ is a $W$-weighted orbital integral, in the same sense, of $g \mapsto \sum_{i \in s} c_i f_i(z \cdot g)$ at $\gamma$, and each $J_i$, $i \in s$, is measurable as a function of $z$.
--
--   This is the canonicity and linearity statement for height-weighted orbital integrals attached to a regular split (diagonal) class: the weight $W$ is invariant under left translation by the centraliser in this case, which allows a common section function to be chosen for a finite linear combination of test functions. It is used in the assembly of the hyperbolic terms of the trace-formula comparison, where the matched combination $\sum_i c_i J_i$ has to be recognised as a single weighted class integrand and where measurability in the central parameter $z$ is needed before integrating over the idele class group.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AutomorphicForm_isWeightedOrbitalIntegralOn_sum_mul_centralScalar_mul_and_measurable_of_diagonal.lean

